  "summary": {
    "id": "sum1",
    "text": "A neural network for recognizing handwritten digits is built from layers of neurons holding activations between 0 and 1, connected by roughly 13,000 weights and biases that learning adjusts.",
    "provenance": "ai",
    "speaker_claim": false,
    "source_refs": [
      {
        "chunk_id": "0ab4a7ae-2858-509d-93f7-b25aebd73a63",
        "char_start": 449,
        "char_end": 525
      },
      {
        "chunk_id": "7bc7797c-8f87-586b-9465-4634ccd22220",
        "char_start": 915,
        "char_end": 978
      }
    ]
  },
  "key_points": [
    {
      "id": "kp1",
      "text": "A neuron is simply a thing that holds a number between 0 and 1, and that number is called its activation.",
      "provenance": "ai",
      "speaker_claim": false,
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "ea2d9379-f63b-5ad9-8481-7fbcc2a2d054",
          "char_start": 465,
          "char_end": 531
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "ea2d9379-f63b-5ad9-8481-7fbcc2a2d054",
          "char_start": 868,
          "char_end": 922
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "kp2",
      "text": "The input layer has one neuron per pixel of the 28x28 image, 784 in total, and the output layer has ten, one per digit.",
      "provenance": "ai",
      "speaker_claim": false,
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "ea2d9379-f63b-5ad9-8481-7fbcc2a2d054",
          "char_start": 691,
          "char_end": 711
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "ea2d9379-f63b-5ad9-8481-7fbcc2a2d054",
          "char_start": 1138,
          "char_end": 1194
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "kp3",
      "text": "Activations in one layer determine the activations of the next, and that is the whole information-processing mechanism.",
      "provenance": "ai",
      "speaker_claim": false,
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "b05adfd8-16d2-56df-b0e3-783b66c4b467",
          "char_start": 684,
          "char_end": 752
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "kp4",
      "text": "Every connection carries a weight, and every neuron adds a bias that sets how high its weighted sum must be before it activates.",
      "provenance": "ai",
      "speaker_claim": false,
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "d525f757-6558-5d8d-9708-3d9282b61b2e",
          "char_start": 368,
          "char_end": 414
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "7bc7797c-8f87-586b-9465-4634ccd22220",
          "char_start": 140,
          "char_end": 249
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "kp5",
      "text": "The sigmoid function squashes an unbounded weighted sum back into the range between 0 and 1.",
      "provenance": "ai",
      "speaker_claim": false,
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "c89a70e8-67f0-52c8-95df-42b2390d6d1c",
          "char_start": 539,
          "char_end": 591
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "c89a70e8-67f0-52c8-95df-42b2390d6d1c",
          "char_start": 383,
          "char_end": 459
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "kp6",
      "text": "Learning means getting the computer to find a valid setting for all of those weights and biases.",
      "provenance": "ai",
      "speaker_claim": false,
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "7bc7797c-8f87-586b-9465-4634ccd22220",
          "char_start": 1084,
          "char_end": 1185
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "important_details": [
    {
      "id": "d1",
      "text": "The demonstrated network uses two hidden layers of sixteen neurons each, which the presenter calls an arbitrary choice.",
      "provenance": "ai",
      "speaker_claim": true,
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "b05adfd8-16d2-56df-b0e3-783b66c4b467",
          "char_start": 317,
          "char_end": 360
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "b05adfd8-16d2-56df-b0e3-783b66c4b467",
          "char_start": 377,
          "char_end": 411
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "d2",
      "text": "Pixel grayscale values run from 0 for black up to 1 for white.",
      "provenance": "ai",
      "speaker_claim": false,
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "ea2d9379-f63b-5ad9-8481-7fbcc2a2d054",
          "char_start": 810,
          "char_end": 866
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "d3",
      "text": "Weight grids are drawn with green pixels for positive weights and red for negative ones.",
      "provenance": "ai",
      "speaker_claim": false,
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "d525f757-6558-5d8d-9708-3d9282b61b2e",
          "char_start": 736,
          "char_end": 822
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "d4",
      "text": "The network's answer is whichever neuron in the output layer is brightest.",
      "provenance": "ai",
      "speaker_claim": false,
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "e909fb8f-b192-575c-b0f3-a38bef4a3b25",
          "char_start": 241,
          "char_end": 306
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "d5",
      "text": "Written compactly, one layer transition is a matrix-vector product plus a bias vector, wrapped in a sigmoid.",
      "provenance": "ai",
      "speaker_claim": false,
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "68d4d73a-3872-58c1-be19-652391e37df5",
          "char_start": 5,
          "char_end": 44
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "68d4d73a-3872-58c1-be19-652391e37df5",
          "char_start": 840,
          "char_end": 902
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "practical_actions": [
    {
      "id": "pa1",
      "text": "Watch the presenter's linear algebra series, especially chapter 3, for the matrix-vector background this relies on.",
      "provenance": "ai",
      "speaker_claim": false,
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "68d4d73a-3872-58c1-be19-652391e37df5",
          "char_start": 593,
          "char_end": 664
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
